+# DOS executable formats, overlays and launch diagnosis
+
+Use this reference before suggesting an extender, modifying a DOS executable,
+selecting COM mode, or treating a game-resource suffix as an architecture.
+Start with the exact installed version, method catalog and source identity.
+A different kit with the same version label need not contain these commands.
+
+## First distinguish the layers
+
+An installer format, executable format, CPU mode, linker/overlay scheme,
+resource container and game scripting engine answer different questions.
+For example: GOG Inno wrapper -> DOS MZ -> x86 real mode -> RTLink overlay
+manager -> MADS resources. A ScummVM engine classification is not an original
+executable loader classification. Neither a studio name nor a similar filename
+establishes a shared interpreter ABI.
+
+Before any repair, record the actual path, file size, SHA-256, first 64 bytes,
+loader result and launcher recipe. A report that an EXE lacks MZ must state
+which bytes were inspected. Check whether the agent opened the wrapper, an
+archive member, a decompressed image, a text listing, or the wrong directory.
+Do not add DOS/4GW because a scan failed to find an expected signature.
+
+## COM: explicit headerless loading
+
+A DOS COM image normally starts at offset 0100h in the PSP segment. It has no
+MZ relocation table. A `.COM` suffix does not establish that loading contract;
+other systems and renamed files can use the same suffix. An unknown headerless
+file might be data, a device driver, an overlay or packed code. In fDisBull,
+`--image com` is an explicit profile selection, not an automatic fallback.
+PSP memory and initial stack space are not bytes recovered from the file.
+
+## MZ: header, resident image and appended material
+
+An old DOS MZ header has 28 defined bytes, followed by relocation entries and
+optional header padding. Validate the entire structure, not just `MZ` at zero.
+The important fields are little-endian words:
+
+| Offset | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| 02h / 04h | Bytes in final page / count of 512-byte pages |
+| 06h / 08h | Relocation count / header size in 16-byte paragraphs |
+| 0Ah / 0Ch | Minimum / maximum additional allocation in paragraphs |
+| 0Eh / 10h | Initial relative SS / SP |
+| 14h / 16h | Initial IP / relative CS |
+| 18h / 1Ah | Relocation-table file offset / DOS overlay number |
+
+Let `H = header_paragraphs * 16`. If the final-page length is nonzero, the
+DOS-declared file extent is `(page_count - 1) * 512 + final_page_length`;
+otherwise it is `page_count * 512`. This extent **includes the header**.
+The resident image occupies `[H, declared_extent)`, not the entire file.
+Initial file-backed entry is `H + initial_CS * 16 + initial_IP`.
+Each relocation record is an offset/segment pair naming a word in the loaded
+image. The runtime loader adds its actual load segment to that word.
+
+An appended tail may contain overlays, resources, debug records, a packer's
+payload or unrelated data. A valid resident image does not classify its tail.
+Preserve the tail and record separate ranges. The MZ overlay-number field is
+not a complete inventory of a linker's proprietary overlay blocks.
+
+### The 003Ch trap
+
+Modern extended headers use a pointer at file offset 003Ch. In an old MZ file,
+that position can instead belong to a relocation table beginning at 001Eh.
+Do not reinterpret relocation bytes as `e_lfanew`. First establish that the
+header actually admits the pointer, validate its range, then validate the
+pointed-to signature and all required tables. An incidental `LE`, `LX`, `NE`
+or `PE` string anywhere in the file is not a format verdict.
+
+## NE, LE, LX and PE: a stub is not the whole executable
+
+NE is a segmented executable format with segment, resource and relocation
+structures. LE/LX use object/page/fixup machinery and can appear with DOS
+extenders or other operating environments. PE has its own COFF/optional-header
+and section structure. Validate the selected format using the existing loader;
+do not change CPU mode merely because the first bytes are MZ.
+
+A 16-bit stub may prepare or reject an execution environment before entering
+another image. Static recognition, mapping support and live execution are
+separate gates. An extender such as DOS/4GW is an execution component, not a
+universal repair blob. Establish the required loader through valid structures,
+original configuration, imports/strings and preferably an observed launch.
+Use the delivered runtime or a documented compatible replacement, never a
+fabricated stub. Packed executables need unpacking evidence, not an arbitrary
+change of disassembler bitness.
+
+## Overlays are not DOS extenders
+
+An overlay manager loads selected code/data blocks into reusable memory regions.
+Multiple different file ranges can occupy the same segment at different times.
+A static offset, even with a segment, is insufficient to identify those bytes.
+Record `(source hash, overlay ID, overlay-relative offset)` for static work.
+A runtime identity additionally needs observed load address, relocated bytes,
+run identity and overlay generation. Preserve unresolved loader calls and
+cross-overlay transfers rather than inventing a single flat address space.
+
+The RTLink commands currently admit the observed **embedded v2** profile:
+
+```text
+fdisbull rtlink-inspect /private/SPHERE.EXE
+fdisbull rtlink-disasm /private/SPHERE.EXE --overlay-id 2 --offset 0 --max-instructions 32
+fdisbull rtlink-extract /private/SPHERE.EXE /private/new-rtlink-export --expected-sha256 <source-sha256>
+```
+
+The inspector checks the resident relocation-derived index, sequential IDs,
+block extents, paragraph/header sizes and per-overlay relocation word bounds.
+Extraction writes separate, unrelocated `.raw` regions and a source-bound
+manifest. These are **private game-derived data**, not distributable fixtures
+and not runnable reconstructed programs. A terminal partial paragraph is
+reported as unavailable; missing bytes are not zero-filled. Linear disassembly
+is candidate decoding, not proof that every decoded byte executes. External
+OVL files and other RTLink variations remain unsupported.
+
+## Reproduce the launch contract before diagnosing the CPU
+
+Read the delivered BAT files and DOSBox configuration. Record the launcher,
+argument tokens, working directory, environment changes, drive letters and
+resource search paths. The launcher and the main engine may be different
+executables. A program can start successfully and then exit because it cannot
+find a CD, archive, index file or configuration. That is not proof of a missing
+extender. Do not recursively disassemble every helper EXE as the game.
+
+The native DOSBox route now accepts explicit argument tokens and one read-only
+Mode-1 CD. Other backends reject these options instead of ignoring them:
+
+```text
+fdisbull debug /private/mainmenu.fdb --executable /tools/dosbox-x --game-dir /private/game/DRAGON --arguments '["-p"]' --cd-image /private/game/GAME.INS
+```
+
+`arguments` is a list of printable ASCII tokens, without whitespace, quotes or
+shell operators. This first profile does not offer arbitrary shell commands or
+space-containing argument quoting. The native PSP tail budget includes the
+leading separator. The source image remains the project-bound EXE/COM, not a
+BAT file. Batch environment changes still need separate explicit handling;
+do not claim that passing `-p` reproduces every possible batch operation.
+
+The CD launcher admits one Mode-1 track with INDEX 01 at zero, no audio tracks
+or generated gaps. It creates an owned canonical `.cue` and a link to the
+fingerprinted image, then issues only its own `imgmount` command. C remains the
+selected game directory. Source metadata/hashes are retained; a D: mapping is
+not reported runtime-verified until a guest witness accesses it. Native machine
+checkpoint capture/restore with this new CD attachment is deliberately refused
+pending media-identity and restore qualification. In-game save/load is distinct.
+
+A GOG `.INS` can be CUE text. `disc-inspect` validates its content and referenced
+files, including legitimate single-digit TRACK/INDEX numbers. It does not run
+the file, trust its host association, or turn arbitrary `.INS` files into discs.
+For raw `.GOG`/`.BIN`, select the measured sector mode; ISO filesystem sectors
+are not necessarily the physical stored-sector size. Preserve audio separately
+when reducing mixed-mode discs; this launch profile does not admit them.
+
+## Stop guessing: decision order and counterchecks
+
+1. Verify exact source identity and structured format admission. Preserve a
+   refusal verbatim with the file offset and missing structure.
+2. Reproduce the original launcher's arguments, directories and media. Run one
+   bounded observation; distinguish entry, menu, input readiness and gameplay.
+3. If native startup is valid but code disappears or changes, investigate
+   overlays, dynamic loading, self-modification or a guest VM at that boundary.
+4. Change the smallest justified tool seam. Test authored bad headers, invalid
+   ranges, stale identities and unsupported backends as well as the real target.
+
+Do not fix a hypothesis by editing the original bytes. Do not equate a header
+parse, successful code decode, screenshot or one menu transition with full-game
+compatibility. Native-original evidence and ScummVM replacement behavior remain
+separately attributed.
+
+## Primary format references
+
+- Microsoft Press, *The MS-DOS Encyclopedia*, Section II (PCjs archival copy):
+  https://www.pcjs.org/documents/books/mspl13/msdos/encyclopedia/section2/
+- Microsoft Press, segmented NE format, Appendix K:
+  https://www.pcjs.org/documents/books/mspl13/msdos/encyclopedia/appendix-k/
+- Microsoft PE specification:
+  https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/debug/pe-format
+- RTLink v2 reference implementation, dreammaster/tools, `rtlink_decode` branch,
+  `rtlink_decode/variation2.cpp`, blob `d6dfccff3c143d04fc169bdf2606b99105413b34`.
+  The upstream tool is a disassembly aid, not a runnable-file repair.
